// The refusals — four kinds, where the package has one `string` and two `i32`s.
//
// `design/system/0009` D4: *"a construct this cannot read is an error naming it and its position.
// Never a guess, never a silent pass-through."* The decision is right and the shipped shape carries
// it as prose:
//
//     export struct Stripped { u8[] out; string error; i32 line; i32 col; }
//     export struct Lexed    { i32[] tokens; string error; i32 errorAt; }
//
// Both hold *"empty when `error` is set"* as a comment, in one package, twice. Each is a `Result`
// whose two halves were written as one struct because there was no other way to answer either
// — and the messages are good English that no caller can act on: an editor integration wanting to
// underline the `enum` keyword has a string beginning with a backtick.

/** A position in the source. Two `i32`s that must come from the same token, so: one value. */
export struct At { i32 line; i32 col; }

/**
 * `enum` and `namespace` — *"it emits a runtime object"*.
 *
 * One variant for the two, because the reason is the same one and the shipped messages are the same
 * sentence with the keyword changed. `what` is the keyword, so a caller still reports which.
 */
export struct EmitsRuntimeValue { string what; At at; }

/** `declare` — *"it has no JavaScript to leave behind"*. A different reason, so a different arm. */
export struct NothingToLeave { At at; }

/**
 * `constructor(private x)` — *"it declares a field as well as a parameter"*.
 *
 * The fourth refusal, and the only one that is not a keyword at the start of a declaration: it is a
 * modifier on a parameter, so a caller cannot find it by grepping. That asymmetry is why the
 * position matters more here than in the other three.
 */
export struct ParameterProperty { At at; }

/** The lexer's own: a token it could not close. */
export struct Unterminated { string what; i32 offset; }

export union<Unterminated> LexFault;
export union<EmitsRuntimeValue, NothingToLeave, ParameterProperty, LexFault> StripFault;

// ## The nested union is on purpose and it is the case the flattening question is about
//
// `StripFault` contains `LexFault`, so `Err(is LexFault):` is one arm for *"it is not valid
// JavaScript, never mind the types"* — the grouping `../../../TECHNICAL.md` says nesting preserves,
// and the members are disjoint, so it is the good case.
//
// `@/packages/box/src/upper.wac` has the bad one: a stacked transform whose error set contains the
// same member at two depths. Both are in this directory now, which is what makes the question
// answerable — and neither is a corner case.
